{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Uptrend underprediction (positive bias needed): When the recent 1–2 week slope is positive, the model systematically undershoots the next ~5–20 trading days by roughly +1 to +3 €/t (largest around days 7–15). Apply an upward level shift proportional to recent momentum.",
    "Turning-point lag: The model reacts late to regime changes. After a breakout to new highs, it keeps forecasting near the prior range for ~1 week; after the peak, it keeps forecasting elevated for too long. Add a momentum/acceleration term to shorten lag (boost early in breakouts; cut faster after peaks).",
    "Post-spike overshoot on long horizon: Following a sharp rise, the model’s far-horizon path stays too high versus realized outcomes (it fails to price the subsequent drawdown). From ~day 20 onward after a spike, apply stronger mean-reversion (downward tilt) rather than maintaining elevated levels.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias pattern: Near-term (days 1–5) errors are smaller; mid-horizon (days 6–15) tends to be too low in rising markets; long-horizon (days 20–30) tends to be too high after peaks. Use piecewise corrections by horizon bucket (1–5, 6–15, 16–30).",
    "Amplitude damping: Forecast volatility is too muted during rallies (misses magnitude of upside) but too persistent after the top (doesn’t damp quickly enough). Increase response to positive shocks early, but increase decay rate of shocks over time (faster fade-out)."
  ]
}
